# November 22, 2026: western and southern Schoharie County, New York: Cobleskill, Richmondville, Summit
# (Charlotteville), Jefferson, Fulton (Breakabeen, West Fulton), Blenheim, South Gilboa, Livingstonville (Broome),
# Beekman Corners and Sharon Springs (Sharon), and one stone tavern in Schoharie village. Drawn at random by
# tools/choose.py --state ny (region scho), which offered Cobleskill; the Atlas had nothing in Cobleskill,
# Richmondville, Jefferson or the Breakabeen valley. Mostly standing buildings: churches, two one-room schools,
# a feed mill and a grist mill, a depot, three hamlets' Register districts and two working inns.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py, including the Schoharie County Register
# list), HMdb markers, the Bull's Head Inn's and the American Hotel's own pages, the Schoharie County Historical
# Society's history of the Town of Cobleskill (theoldstonefort.org), the Town of Summit historian's schoolhouse PDF
# on schohariecounty-ny.gov, Mohawk Valley Museums' and Pure Catskills' pages for the Jefferson Historical Society.
# Every Register form tried (05000665, 00000090, 12001261, 07001370, 08000864, 06000575, 06000894, 12000481,
# 76001276) is an NPGallery placeholder.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HM(m,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+m]

BATCH={'key':'scho_phfb','label':'Western Schoharie County, NY: Cobleskill, Richmondville, Jefferson and the Breakabeen valley','added':'2026-11-22 04:57'}

PLACES=[
N('cobleskill-historic-district','Cobleskill village: Main Street, the millpond park and the Hotel Augustan','Cobleskill','scho','town','standing',42.67806,-74.48417,'area','public','walk','','1802–1920s',
  'Cobleskill grew where the Loonenburg Turnpike crossed the Cobleskill Creek, and later round its railroad. The Register district listed in 1978 takes in 180 contributing buildings and eight sites: the business blocks, several residential streets, churches, an old school, the railroad and the fairgrounds, with a park and millpond on a small stream. Its focal point is the Hotel Augustan of 1874, later turned to commercial use; its oldest building is the Bull\'s Head Inn of 1802.',
  'Walk Main Street for the commercial blocks and the 1874 Hotel Augustan, and the park with the millpond. The Hotel Augustan is red brick with a long porch on its street front (a 2008 photograph on Wikimedia Commons). The town marker, the war memorial and the World Wars memorial clock stand together at the centre (HMdb). Which buildings survive in what state, and the fabric of each, was not checked building by building; interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public village streets owned by the village; the houses and shops are private. Street parking in the village (not checked).',
  [W('Cobleskill Historic District'),L('Schoharie County Historical Society: A History of the Town of Cobleskill','https://theoldstonefort.org/?p=4771'),HM('115741','Town of Cobleskill')],brick='some'),
N('bulls-head-inn-cobleskill','Bull\'s Head Inn','Cobleskill','scho','hotel','standing',42.6775,-74.48546,'exact','open','drive','','1802',
  'The oldest building in Cobleskill, built in 1802 as an inn and tavern for traffic on the Loonenburg Turnpike, on a site where a log cabin of 1752 and two later buildings had each burned (the first in the 1778 raid on Cobleskill). The inn\'s own history names Seth Wakeman as its builder; the Schoharie County Historical Society\'s town history says a Lawyer had it built after the earlier building burned in 1802. Charles Courter bought it in 1839 and it was a private house, the Courter Mansion, until 1966, when it reopened as a restaurant. It closed after the 2011 storms, and its first and second floors were restored and reopened in June 2015.',
  'The 1802 inn on Park Place, restored in 2014–2015 to its early look; a working restaurant, with the Cellar Tavern (built 1982, rebuilt in 2017 from reclaimed old materials) below. The inn calls itself haunted and is on the state\'s Haunted History Trail. What original interior fabric survives was not checked.',
  'A turnpike tavern of 1802 that is still serving.',
  'A privately owned restaurant at 105 Park Place; dining by reservation (telephone on its page). Hours from the inn\'s own location page, read November 2026: the restaurant and the Cellar Tavern keep different hours, and Monday is closed. Parking not checked.',
  [L('Bull\'s Head Inn: About','https://bullsheadinncobleskill.getbento.com/about'),L('Bull\'s Head Inn: hours and location','https://bullsheadinncobleskill.getbento.com/location/bulls-head-inn/'),L('Schoharie County Historical Society: A History of the Town of Cobleskill','https://theoldstonefort.org/?p=4771'),W('Cobleskill Historic District')],
  brick='?',hours='Restaurant Tuesday to Saturday 11:30 a.m. to 8 p.m.; Cellar Tavern Tuesday to Sunday from 4 p.m. (Saturday and Sunday from 11:30 a.m.); closed Monday'),
N('bramanville-mill-cobleskill','Bramanville Mill (Chickering Grist Mill)','Cobleskill','scho','industry','standing',42.68806,-74.40611,'area','view','drive','',  '',
  'Bramanville grew up round mills on the Cobleskill Creek east of the village: a fulling mill from 1816, then saw, grist and cider mills, in a settlement first called Brayman\'s Mills after John Brayman, its chief businessman. The grist mill, also called the Chickering Grist Mill, is a 30 by 50 foot gable-roofed frame building, two and a half stories on the end facing the road. Listed on the National Register in 1976.',
  'The frame mill end-on to the road. Its date of building, whether any machinery or the race survives, and its present use were not found in any source read; whether it still stands rests on the aerial alone (see the pin note). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private; view from the road only. Owner and parking not checked.',
  [W('Bramanville Mill'),L('Schoharie County Historical Society: A History of the Town of Cobleskill','https://theoldstonefort.org/?p=4771')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('richmondville-methodist-church','Richmondville United Methodist Church','Richmondville','scho','worship','standing',42.63455,-74.56516,'exact','view','drive','','1893–about 1900',
  'A nearly square frame church at 266 Main Street with an engaged entrance and bell tower, built about 1900, beside a two-story frame parsonage with gable and hipped roofs built in 1893. Listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'The square church with its corner tower and the parsonage beside it. Interior, glass and woodwork not checked; no page of the congregation\'s own giving service times was found.',
  '',
  'An active congregation of the Upper New York Conference (its conference page lists the church). View from Main Street (State Route 7); service times not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Richmondville United Methodist Church'),L('Upper New York Conference: Richmondville UMC','https://www.unyumc.org/resources/church/richmondville-umc')],brick='?'),
N('bunn-tillapaugh-feed-mill','Bunn–Tillapaugh Feed Mill','Richmondville','scho','industry','standing',42.63306,-74.56389,'site','view','drive','','1885',
  'A feed mill of 1885 in the village of Richmondville: a 40 by 60 foot, four-story heavy timber frame in narrow clapboards, set on a stone basement story built into the steep bank of Bear Gulch, a branch of the Cobleskill Creek, with its mill pond beside it. Listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'The tall clapboarded mill on its stone basement against the Bear Gulch bank, and the mill pond. Present use and machinery not checked. HMdb markers in the village mark the sites of earlier mills that are gone: Asa Bailey\'s grist mill of 1807 on Mill Street and the paper mill of 1865–1883 at the end of Holmes Street.',
  '',
  'Private; view from the street. Owner and parking not checked.',
  [W('Bunn–Tillapaugh Feed Mill'),HM('156504','Grist Mill'),HM('157701','Old Paper Mill')],brick='no'),
N('charlotteville-schoolhouse','Charlotteville District 14 schoolhouse','Charlotteville','scho','civic','standing',42.54586,-74.66554,'site','view','drive','','1894–1951',
  'The last one-room school in the Town of Summit to close, Charlotteville District 14 taught from 1894 to 1951; the town historian notes that it is in fact a two-room building, the only one in Summit. Across the road stood District 4, whose deed is the oldest on record in the town. The District 14 school stands on its own site, refurbished, and a marker put up by the Charlotteville Schoolhouse Restoration Committee names it.',
  'The refurbished schoolhouse and its marker at Charlotte Valley Road (State Route 6). Whether it opens to visitors was not found; interior not checked. Charlotteville\'s seminary of the 1850s is gone: markers nearby mark its site.',
  '',
  'Restored by a local committee; owner and any opening days not checked. View from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [L('Town of Summit historian: Summit one-room schoolhouses (PDF)','https://www4.schohariecounty-ny.gov/PdfFiles/TSUM/History/SummitOneRoomSchoolhouses.pdf'),HM('246170','Charlotteville Schoolhouse'),HM('149533','Site of Charlotteville Seminary')],brick='?'),
N('maple-museum-jefferson','First Presbyterian Church of Jefferson (the Maple Museum)','Jefferson','scho','worship','standing',42.48056,-74.61333,'site','open','drive','','1837',
  'A Greek Revival meeting-house church of 1837: two stories, heavy timber frame, flush board siding, a Doric pediment and entablature, and a square staged bell tower on the front. The church closed in 1919 and the building was afterwards a school, an American Legion hall, and then a community centre and local history museum, the Maple Museum, restored by the Jefferson Historical Society. Listed on the National Register in 2012.',
  'The flush-boarded temple front and the staged tower on the village street. The society uses it for concerts, programs and exhibits; what original interior fabric survives was not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned and used by the Jefferson Historical Society (163 Main Street, Jefferson). Hours as the society\'s own listing on Pure Catskills gives them, read November 2026; not on a page of the society\'s own site. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First Presbyterian Church of Jefferson'),L('Pure Catskills: Jefferson Historical Society','https://purecatskills.com/member/jefferson-historical-society'),L('Mohawk Valley Museums: Jefferson Historical Society','https://mohawkvalleymuseums.us/browse-all/schoharie-county/jefferson-historical-society/')],
  brick='no',hours='Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m., Memorial Day to Labor Day, and special events'),
N('judd-hall-jefferson','Judd Hall (Masonic Lodge 554) and the Jefferson village green','Jefferson','scho','civic','standing',42.4815,-74.6111,'exact','view','walk','','1866',
  'A meeting hall used by Masonic Lodge 554 from 1866, and from 1905 also by an Order of the Eastern Star chapter, bought by the Jefferson Historical Society in 2017 and named Judd Hall; the society restores it for public events and plans a local history museum upstairs. Along the same street are the village green with the town marker and war memorial, a pair of old mill stones, and the state marker for the Jefferson Academy of 1812, one of the first schools of its kind in the county built by public subscription.',
  'Judd Hall at 163 Main Street, with its Pomeroy marker; the green and the mill stones a short walk west. Whether the academy building itself survives is not said by its marker and was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Judd Hall belongs to the Jefferson Historical Society; the green is public. The society\'s listing gives open hours for its buildings on summer Saturdays without saying which building; check before going. Street parking (not checked).',
  [HM('205535','Masonic Lodge 554'),HM('137527','Jefferson Academy'),HM('137630','Mill Stones'),HM('137597','Village Green'),L('Pure Catskills: Jefferson Historical Society','https://purecatskills.com/member/jefferson-historical-society')],brick='?'),
N('breakabeen-historic-district','Breakabeen hamlet','Breakabeen','scho','town','standing',42.52446,-74.40985,'area','view','drive','','early 1800s–1870s',
  'A hamlet in the Town of Fulton on the Schoharie Creek, on ground fought over in the Revolution: part of it was destroyed by British forces in 1780. The Register district listed in 1974 holds 33 buildings, most of the early and middle 1800s in a vernacular Greek Revival, several of them temple-fronted with one- or two-story porticoes on square columns. Its most unusual building is a hotel built after the Civil War, a large square two-story building with a flat roof in a version of the Italian villa style.',
  'Drive or walk State Route 30 through the hamlet for the temple-fronted houses, the Presbyterian church and the flat-roofed villa hotel. The Schoharie Creek flooded the valley in Tropical Storm Irene in August 2011; which buildings came through, and in what state, was not checked. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public road through private houses; view from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Breakabeen Historic District'),W('Breakabeen, New York')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('west-fulton-methodist-church','West Fulton Methodist Church','West Fulton','scho','worship','standing',42.56369,-74.46172,'exact','view','drive','','1906',
  'A two-story Late Victorian frame church of 1906 in the hamlet of West Fulton, with a broad front gable on a limestone foundation and a many-staged bell tower set to one side of the front. Its interior follows the Akron plan. Listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'The broad gabled front and the off-centre tower. The Akron-plan interior is the thing to see inside if it opens; no service times or visiting hours were found, and the interior was not checked.',
  '',
  'View from the road; whether the congregation still meets was not found. Parking not checked.',
  [W('West Fulton Methodist Church')],brick='?'),
N('north-blenheim-historic-district','North Blenheim hamlet and its 1841 church','North Blenheim','scho','town','standing',42.47075,-74.44499,'area','view','drive','','1841 and earlier',
  'The hamlet on the Schoharie Creek at the Old Blenheim Bridge. The Register district listed in 1974 holds 25 buildings and a site, most showing a vernacular Greek Revival, with an exceptional Greek Revival church of 1841. The creek\'s 500-year flood in Tropical Storm Irene on August 28, 2011 lifted the covered bridge off its abutments and swept it away, as the town\'s markers at the bridge site tell; the bridge has its own entry on the Atlas.',
  'The 1841 Greek Revival church and the houses along State Route 30. What the 2011 flood left of the district\'s buildings was not checked building by building; interiors not checked. The bridge markers (The Creation, The Life, The Floods, The Re-Creation) stand on Eastside Road.',
  '',
  'Public road through private houses; view from the road. Parking at the bridge site on Eastside Road (see the bridge entry; not checked here).',
  [W('North Blenheim Historic District'),W('Blenheim, New York'),HM('192772','The Floods')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('forks-in-the-road-schoolhouse','Forks in the Road Schoolhouse','South Gilboa','scho','civic','standing',42.412,-74.56284,'site','view','drive','','1849–1930s',
  'A one-room school of 1849 at South Gilboa: one story, gable roofed and timber framed. It held classes into the 1930s, and its privy still stands with it. Listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'The small gabled schoolhouse and the privy. Owner and any opening days not found; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'View from the road. Owner and parking not checked.',
  [W('Forks in the Road Schoolhouse')],brick='?'),
N('south-gilboa-station','South Gilboa station (Ulster and Delaware Railroad)','South Gilboa','scho','rail','standing',42.41022,-74.55803,'site','view','drive','','about 1905–1954',
  'One of the Ulster and Delaware Railroad\'s prefabricated stations of the early 1900s, at milepost 70.6, about a quarter mile east of the first South Gilboa station, which had a long platform for ice cut on Mayham Pond and shipped to ice houses in Kingston. The station was a good freight stop for the farms round it. Passenger service on the line ended on March 31, 1954; the station was abandoned and left to decay. Wikipedia says the Town of Gilboa Historical Society plans to restore it. Listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'The small frame depot beside the old rail bed. Its condition today, and whether the restoration has been done, were not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner and access not checked; view from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('South Gilboa station')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('livingstonville-community-church','Livingstonville Community Church','Livingstonville','scho','worship','standing',42.48816,-74.26825,'exact','view','drive','','1849',
  'A plain Greek Revival church of 1849 at 1667 Hauverville Road in Livingstonville, in the Town of Broome: rectangular and gable roofed, with an engaged clapboarded bell tower topped by an open belfry. Listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'The clapboarded front with its tower and open belfry. No service times or visiting hours found; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'View from the road. Whether the church still holds services was not found. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Livingstonville Community Church')],brick='?'),
N('st-johns-lutheran-beekman-corners','Zion St. John\'s Lutheran Church, Beekman Corners','Beekman Corners','scho','worship','standing',42.74111,-74.58556,'site','view','drive','','1860–1861',
  'The congregation, founded in 1745, was the oldest in Schoharie County; its first church was finished about 1787. The present church was built in 1860–1861 from material salvaged from the first: a timber frame in narrow clapboards, vernacular Greek Revival, with a staged tower rising to an open belfry on plain Tuscan columns, added with its bell about ten years later. The congregation dwindled, joined Zion Church of Seward in 1997, and held its last service on July 10, 2011; in 2016 the bell went to the Sharon Historical Society, which shows it outside its museum. Listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'The clapboarded church with its open columned belfry, now without its bell; the 1936 state marker for the first Lutheran congregation in the county stands on State Route 10 nearby. The bell can be seen outside the Sharon Historical Society museum at 238 Main Street, Sharon Springs. Owner and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'No longer used for services (last one in 2011). View from the road; owner not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('St. John\'s Lutheran Church (Beekman Corners, New York)'),HM('158249','First Lutheran Congregation'),L('Mohawk Valley Museums: Sharon Historical Society','https://mohawkvalleymuseums.us/museums/sharon-historical-society/')],brick='no'),
N('american-hotel-sharon-springs','American Hotel','Sharon Springs','scho','hotel','standing',42.79378,-74.61743,'exact','open','drive','hotels','about 1840–1851',
  'A large Greek Revival frame hotel of the spa village, with a recessed two-story porch behind a colonnade of eight Doric pillars. Its date differs between sources: the hotel\'s own history says Nicholas LaRue of Esperance built it about 1840, while Wikipedia gives 1847 to 1851. It ran as a hotel until the 1950s, then served as a religious retreat for Jewish congregations, and stood abandoned for close to 30 years before Doug Plummer and Garth Roberts bought the decaying building in 1996; after five years of work it reopened on May 23, 2001. New owners took it on in 2025. Listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'The eight-pillared two-story porch on Main Street, inside the Sharon Springs Historic District. A working inn of nine guest rooms and a restaurant; what original interior fabric survives was not checked.',
  'A spa hotel saved after thirty empty years.',
  'A privately owned inn and restaurant at 192 Main Street; rooms and tables by reservation (telephone on its page). Hours from the hotel\'s own home page, read November 2026. Parking not checked.',
  [W('American Hotel (Sharon Springs, New York)'),L('American Hotel: History','https://www.americanhotelny.com/history/'),L('American Hotel','https://www.americanhotelny.com/')],
  brick='no',hours='Restaurant dinner Thursday to Monday, reservations 5 to 8 p.m., pub from 4 p.m.; brunch Saturday and Sunday 11 a.m. to 2 p.m.; inn rooms by reservation'),
N('swarts-tavern-schoharie','Swarts Tavern (old stone house)','Schoharie','scho','hotel','standing',42.67428,-74.3049,'exact','view','drive','','about 1772',
  'A stone house built about 1772 and used for many years as a tavern. On October 17, 1780, while the family sheltered in the Old Stone Fort, raiders set it on fire and Patriot soldiers put the fire out. Peter P. Snyder owned it in 1803 and Lodowick Fries in 1833. Schoharie County\'s only duel, between Philip Schuyler 2nd and Josiah Clark, was fought behind it in 1820. Two state markers of 1932 and 1933 stand in front.',
  'The stone house at 455 Main Street (State Route 30) with its two markers. It is a private home: look from the road. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1772 tavern that survived the 1780 raid.',
  'A private residence (HMdb). View from the street only. Street parking in the village (not checked).',
  [HM('32197','Swarts Tavern'),HM('145449','Old Stone House')],brick='no'),
]

